1
00:00:01,424 --> 00:00:09,307
Welcome to Asia episode two of seven. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,959 --> 00:00:28,210
Seven places take us from the eastern Mediterranean to Iran: Syria, Lebanon,
Israel, Palestine, Jordan, Iraq and Iran. Map outlines simplify disputed
boundaries and do not show current control.

3
00:00:28,896 --> 00:00:32,948
Let's bring Syria and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:37,944 --> 00:00:49,032
Syria lies below Türkiye and west of Iraq. It has a short Mediterranean coast,
with Lebanon and Israel toward its southwestern edge and Jordan farther south.

5
00:00:49,684 --> 00:01:02,014
Palmyra developed as an oasis city on caravan routes through the desert. Its
architecture brought together influences from the Mediterranean world and
lands farther east.

6
00:01:02,674 --> 00:01:15,422
Picture an oasis crossroads between coast and inland desert. Syria sits above
Jordan and west of Iraq; the trade routes help connect that position with its
history.

7
00:01:16,124 --> 00:01:19,270
Let's bring Lebanon and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:23,505 --> 00:01:33,060
Lebanon is the narrow Mediterranean country below Syria and above Israel. Its
small area still contains a coastal strip and high mountain country.

9
00:01:33,719 --> 00:01:45,828
Ancient Tyre was a Phoenician maritime city whose colonies included Carthage.
A small stretch of coast could connect with a trading network reaching far
across the Mediterranean.

10
00:01:46,485 --> 00:01:57,782
Remember Lebanon as the thin coastal link between Syria and Israel. Imagine
ships leaving Tyre: its maritime history belongs on this western edge of Asia.

11
00:02:00,429 --> 00:02:15,185
Israel lies on the eastern Mediterranean, below Lebanon and west of Jordan.
The Palestinian West Bank and Gaza Strip are separate map areas; borders and
territorial status require care.

12
00:02:15,838 --> 00:02:29,491
Ancient settlement mounds at Megiddo, Hazor and Beer Sheba preserve elaborate
underground water systems. Engineers brought water to towns long before modern
pumps and pipes.

13
00:02:30,152 --> 00:02:45,466
Use the Mediterranean coast and Jordan to orient Israel, with the Red Sea at
its southern tip. The underground-water hook belongs to ancient towns, not to
a claim about disputed boundaries.

14
00:02:48,038 --> 00:03:01,134
GeoClick’s Palestine target includes the West Bank, beside Jordan, and Gaza on
the Mediterranean. The two areas are geographically separate; this simplified
map does not show control on the ground.

15
00:03:01,789 --> 00:03:18,507
At Battir, stone farming terraces and spring-fed channels support olives,
vines and gardens. Families traditionally share water through an organised
distribution system.

16
00:03:19,170 --> 00:03:34,658
Remember two separated areas, and picture terraced hills in the West Bank.
Palestine received United Nations non-member observer State status in 2012;
its borders and sovereignty remain contested.

17
00:03:35,353 --> 00:03:40,171
Let's bring Jordan and its neighbours into view.

18
00:03:42,844 --> 00:03:52,178
Jordan sits east of Israel and the West Bank, below Syria and west of Saudi
Arabia. Its narrow outlet at Aqaba reaches the Red Sea.

19
00:03:52,844 --> 00:04:05,557
Petra was a Nabataean caravan city, with monumental facades carved directly
into rock. Its location connected routes between Arabia, Egypt and the eastern
Mediterranean.

20
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:16,286
Picture the rock-cut city inland, east of the Jordan valley. Jordan is mostly
inland, but keep that small Red Sea opening at its southwestern corner.

21
00:04:17,119 --> 00:04:19,882
Let's bring Iraq and its neighbours into view.

22
00:04:28,084 --> 00:04:40,158
Iraq lies between Syria and Iran, below Türkiye and above Kuwait. The Tigris
and Euphrates cross its territory toward a short coast at the head of the
Gulf.

23
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:52,802
Babylon’s ruins lie south of Baghdad. Its walls, gates and palaces preserve
the remains of a city that became the capital of the Neo-Babylonian Empire.

24
00:04:53,455 --> 00:05:05,866
Remember Iraq through its two great rivers, with Babylon as the historical
anchor. Keep Iran to the east: the similar names belong to different
countries.

25
00:05:08,237 --> 00:05:20,857
Iran is the broad country east of Iraq, with the Caspian Sea to its north and
the Gulf and Gulf of Oman to its south. Afghanistan and Pakistan lie to the
east.

26
00:05:21,518 --> 00:05:34,568
Darius the First founded Persepolis in 518 BC. Its palace complex rose on an
enormous terrace, giving ancient Persian imperial ceremonies a monumental
setting.

27
00:05:35,222 --> 00:05:46,519
Picture the palace terrace between northern mountains and southern seas. Iraq
is the river country to the west; Iran is the much broader neighbour to its
east.

28
00:05:53,031 --> 00:06:09,627
Start with Syria above the small Mediterranean neighbours. Lebanon and Israel
follow the coast; Palestine has the separate West Bank and Gaza areas. Jordan
is inland to their east, with a small Red Sea opening.

29
00:06:10,292 --> 00:06:27,010
Then follow the Tigris and Euphrates through Iraq, with broad Iran to the
east. Recall caravan routes, Phoenician ships, water engineering, farming
terraces, Petra, Babylon and Persepolis.
